# Line-ending policy.
#
# Why this file has to exist: without it, what a .bat file looks like after a
# clone depends on the CLONER's `core.autocrlf`. Git for Windows defaults it to
# true (CRLF on checkout), but Linux/macOS default to false (LF) — and a .bat
# with LF-only endings is misread by cmd.exe: `goto :label` lands on the wrong
# line and multi-line blocks collapse. A batch script that works on the author's
# machine then fails on a fresh clone, with no error that points at line endings.
#
# `.gitattributes` is committed, so it overrides each cloner's local setting and
# every checkout gets the same bytes.

# Batch files MUST be CRLF — cmd.exe requires it.
#
# `-text` (binary) rather than `text eol=crlf`: with `eol=crlf` the repository
# stores LF and only the CHECKOUT converts, so a `git clone` is fine but a
# GitHub "Download ZIP" — which serves the stored bytes with no smudge filter —
# hands out LF .bat files that cmd.exe misreads. `-text` stores the CRLF bytes
# as-is, so clone, ZIP and the working tree all agree.
#
# The exchange is that git no longer normalises these files, so the committed
# bytes are whatever was committed. `bat-scripts.test.ts` asserts CRLF on disk,
# which now also guards what gets committed.
*.bat -text

# Shell scripts MUST be LF. A CRLF shebang breaks with "bad interpreter:
# /bin/sh^M: no such file or directory", which is the classic symptom.
*.sh text eol=lf

# The engine's own sources: normalise on commit, native endings on checkout.
*.ts text
*.json text
*.md text

# Binary — never touch these, or a checkout would corrupt them.
*.exe binary
*.ico binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
*.asar binary
